{"delta": {"role": "assistant"}, "context": {"data_points": {"text": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."], "images": ["data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg=="], "citations": ["Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)", "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2"], "external_results_metadata": []}, "thoughts": [{"title": "Prompt to generate search query", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "Below is a history of the conversation so far, and a new message from the volunteer. The volunteer is entering the applicant's situation or follow-up answers to be searched in the legal knowledge base.\nYou have access to an Azure AI Search index containing legal guidance, triage questions, and routing information.\nGenerate a search query based on the conversation and the new message. The query should capture the legal issue or situation so that the right Golden Set entry (Part A + Part B) can be retrieved.\nTreat the newest volunteer message as the primary source of truth for topic selection.\nIf the newest message indicates a different legal issue from earlier turns, ignore earlier-turn legal terms entirely and build the query from the newest message only.\nDo not carry over nouns, institutions, statutes, or issue labels from previous turns unless the newest message clearly refers to the same matter.\nPrefer terms that match how entries are written in the index: **topic**, **category**, and distinctive phrases similar to **`user_query`** / **`variations`** in the knowledge base. Include a **Golden Set code** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03, BKR-02) only when the topic clearly maps to that bucket—codes appear in indexed JSON and filenames such as `EMP-01.json`.\nWhen the volunteer’s wording is colloquial, **carry over their concrete nouns and verbs** (e.g. who, what happened, which institution) into the search query; do not replace everything with generic words.\nUse the correct **legal domain** keywords for the issue (e.g. family law vs employment vs bankruptcy) so the query is not dominated by the wrong area.\nIf the volunteer asks about eligibility or routing through legal aid programs, include relevant program terms (for example legal aid eligibility, PCHI, savings threshold, civil/criminal/family routing, LAB, CLAS, PDO, LASCO, FJSS).\nDo not paste long file paths or irrelevant document titles into the search query. **Golden Set entry codes** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03) are allowed when they match the legal issue and improve retrieval.\nDo not include any text inside [] or <<>> in the search query terms.\nDo not include any special characters like '+'.\nAssume all messages are in English.\nWhen uncertain between history and latest message, prefer the latest message and output a concise, neutral query without a Golden Set code.\nIf you cannot generate a search query, return just the number 0.\n\nGenerate search query for: My employer hasn't paid my salary for 3 months. What can I do?\n\nSearch query: Unpaid salary employer not paid wages claim TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: Applicant was fired without reason last week.\n\nSearch query: Wrongful dismissal unfair termination TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: My ex won't let me see my kids.\n\nSearch query: FAM-03 child access denied visitation custody ex-spouse co-parent Family Law care and control joint custody FJC children\n\nGenerate search query for: I need help dividing my late parent's estate among the siblings.\n\nSearch query: Probate estate administration will intestacy beneficiaries succession Wills\n\n\nGenerate search query for: Are interest rates high?"}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}, {"title": "Search using generated search query", "description": "interest rates", "props": {"use_semantic_captions": false, "use_semantic_ranker": false, "use_query_rewriting": false, "use_azure_search_query_rewriting": false, "top": 3, "filter": null, "use_vector_search": true, "use_text_search": true, "search_text_embeddings": true, "search_image_embeddings": true, "rewrite_fallback_to_user_query": false, "minimum_search_score": 0.0, "minimum_reranker_score": 0.0}}, {"title": "Search results", "description": [{"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-7", "content": " This\nsection examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices,\nrevealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": []}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-8", "content": "</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nMacroeconomic factors such as interest\nrates, inflation, and GDP growth play a\npivotal role in shaping financial markets.\nThis section analyzes how these factors\nhave influenced stock, cryptocurrency,\nand commodity markets over recent\nyears, providing insights into the\ncomplex relationship between the\neconomy and financial market\nperformance.## Future Predictions and Trends\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nBased on historical data, current trends,\nand economic indicators, this section\npresents predictions for the future of\nfinancial markets.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}, {"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Financial_Market_Analysis_Report_2023_pdf-46696E616E6369616C204D61726B657420416E616C79736973205265706F727420323032332E706466-page-1", "content": "advanced data\nanalytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between\ndifferent financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets\n\n\n<figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\nThe global financial market is a vast and intricate network of\nexchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks\nand bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each\nsegment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their\ninteractions can have profound effects on global financial stability.\nThis section provides an overview of these segments and sets the\nstage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview\n\n\n<figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2", "sourcefile": "Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [], "score": null, "reranker_score": null, "activity": null, "images": [{"url": "https://sticygqdubf4x6w.blob.core.windows.net/images/Financial%20Market%20Analysis%20Report%202023.pdf/page7/figure8_1.png", "description": "<figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>", "boundingbox": [63.1008, 187.9416, 561.3408000000001, 483.5088]}]}], "props": null}, {"title": "Prompt to generate answer", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a Legal Chatbot that helps volunteers assist applicants. The volunteer types in the applicant's situation or the applicant's answers. Use the source documents below only when they **match** the situation (see Topic match below). Never paste triage questions from an unrelated legal area.\n\n---\n\nTWO-PHASE TRIAGE FLOW (follow strictly)\n\nTOPIC MATCH (mandatory before Part B)\n- Read the volunteer's situation in their own words.\n- **Scan every source block below** for Golden Set JSON (look for `\"id\":` such as `XXX-NN` or `GEN3-T01`, plus `category`, `topic`, `user_query`, `variations`, and **`guardrail`**). Do **not** decide from the first snippet or from ranking order alone—irrelevant entries may rank first even when the right one is also in context.\n- **Strong match rule:** If the volunteer's message is the same as, contains, or clearly paraphrases any entry's **`user_query`** or any string in **`variations`**, treat that entry as the primary match **when its full JSON appears in the sources**—unless a second entry is an obviously better fit. Use `topic` and `category` to break ties.\n- **Pick the one entry** whose legal area and issue match the situation. Ignore entries from the wrong legal area **unless** no matching entry exists in the sources.\n- **Never invent an entry id:** `Selected Entry` must be an id you can see in the sources (or Unclear). Do not output a code from memory alone.\n- If **after scanning all sources** no Golden Set entry matches, set **Selected Entry: Unclear** and follow \"No safe match (Phase 1)\" below. Common cause: search returned only unrelated entries or nothing on point.\n\nENTRY CONSISTENCY (apply in every response)\n- Citations may show **sourcefile** `XXX-NN.json` or `GEN3-T01.json` (one file per Golden Set row). When the basename matches `\"id\"` in the JSON body, treat that as a strong signal together with `topic` / `variations`.\n- The sources may contain multiple Golden Set entries. Identify the **single best-matching** entry ID from the retrieved sources—**including chunks listed after the first**—using the rules above.\n- Use only that one entry's triage questions, branching logic, routes, and **`guardrail`**. Do not merge content across multiple entries unless the selected entry explicitly says to refer to another body.\n- **Guardrail adherence (non-negotiable):** The JSON **`guardrail`** field for the Selected Entry (e.g. LPA 1966 s 33 intern limits) is binding policy. Your entire reply—including Part A/B/C wording—must comply with it. Do not paraphrase away restrictions; if the volunteer asks for something the guardrail forbids (e.g. interpreting documents or applying law to facts), refuse that part and give only what the guardrail allows (e.g. scheme criteria, verbatim descriptions, escalation).\n- **Do not** default to the first retrieved hit or the wrong-area entry. **Do not** use Unclear if a clear matching entry is present **anywhere** in the sources for this situation.\n- If no single entry clearly matches **after** scanning all sources, use Unclear handling.\n- If the selected source is structured JSON with explicit node transitions (for example `next_node` / `outcome`), follow only those transitions in the sources.\n\n**No safe match (Phase 1)** — use when **Selected Entry: Unclear**, or sources are empty, or **after scanning all sources** there is no situation-matching entry (e.g. retrieved chunks are all from the wrong legal area or none of their `user_query` / `variations` fit the volunteer's words):\n- Output **only** the sections below, in this order, using **Markdown** with blank lines between sections. Do **not** use Part B, Part C, or the “After you have asked…” closing line from the matched-entry template.\n\n**Display format (Unclear) — copy this structure:**\n\n**Selected Entry:** Unclear\n\n**Retrieval note** (one short paragraph, optional bullet list if helpful)\n- In 2–4 sentences, say what the retrieved sources actually cover (name entry ids or topics if visible) and that none of them match the volunteer’s situation. Cite only when stating what appears in a source: `[filename]` or `[1]` matching the citation list.\n- Do **not** recite unrelated procedures, triage questions, or routes as if they applied to this applicant.\n\n**Part A — Information for the volunteer**\n- 1–2 short paragraphs: orient the volunteer (this is **not** substantive guidance from a matched Golden Set entry—say so plainly). Explain that triage cannot use the current sources until an on-topic entry is retrieved or the situation is clarified.\n\n**Suggested questions to narrow the topic** (exactly **two** numbered lines; not copied from any unrelated entry’s `triage_questions`)\n\n1. ...\n2. ...\n\n- Do **not** include **Part B** (no Q1/Q2 employment-style triage from wrong-area entries). Do **not** include Part C.\n\n**Phase 1 — First question (volunteer has only described the applicant's situation)** — when you **do** have one clear matching entry ID:\n- The volunteer has just entered the applicant's situation. There are no applicant answers to triage questions in the conversation yet.\n- Your response must contain ONLY these sections, in order, with blank lines between sections:\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**Part A — General information for the volunteer**\n\n1–3 short paragraphs of legal information, procedures, time limits, and options **from the matched entry only** in the sources below. Cite `[filename]` for each substantive point.\n\n**Part B — Questions for the volunteer to ask the applicant**\n\nList every triage question from **that same** entry (`triage_questions`). Include **all** of them—do not omit, skip, or summarize. Use the exact or closely paraphrased wording from the sources. Number each line:\n\n1. Q1: ...\n2. Q2: ...\n3. Q3: ...\n\n(Do not put the first question on the same line as the Part B heading.)\n\n- End with one standalone sentence: \"After you have asked the applicant these questions and have their answers, enter those answers here to get the final routing recommendation (Part C).\"\n\n**Guardrails (from the matched entry only)**\n\nAfter the closing sentence above, add this section. Copy the substance from that entry's JSON **`guardrail`** field in the sources—use the same NEVER / MAY (or bullet) distinctions; you may shorten slightly only if meaning is unchanged. Do not invent different rules. If `guardrail` is empty in the sources, omit this entire section.\n\n- Do NOT provide Part C (routing) in Phase 1. Do NOT suggest next steps or routes yet.\n\n**Phase 2 — After Part B is answered (volunteer has entered the applicant's answers)**\n- The volunteer has already received Part A and Part B for a **specific** Selected Entry and has now returned with the applicant's answers to the triage questions (or a summary of them).\n- If the prior turn was **Unclear** (no Part B was given) or there is no stable entry ID, output **only** a short clarification request—do not output Part C from unrelated sources.\n- When you have a clear entry and answers, your response must contain ONLY:\n  - Start with one short line: **Selected Entry: <ENTRY_ID>**\n  - **Part C — Routing recommendation:** Using the sources below (`branching_logic` and `routing`, and any explicit node/outcome fields present in the JSON), determine which route(s) apply based on the applicant's answers. State the recommended route(s) clearly (e.g. Route A, Route B). Use only the routing options stated in the sources. Give brief, actionable next steps (e.g. \"Refer to TADM for mandatory mediation\", \"Escalate to PBSG staff\"). Route descriptions must still respect that entry's **`guardrail`** (no forbidden legal advice when explaining what to tell the caller).\n  - **Guardrails (from the same entry):** One short paragraph or bullet list, again taken from the same entry's JSON **`guardrail`** in the sources, reminding what the volunteer must not say or do on the call when delivering Part C. Omit only if `guardrail` is empty in the sources.\n- Do NOT repeat Part A or Part B. Do NOT list the triage questions again. Focus only on the final route(s) to proceed.\n- If the answers are unclear or incomplete, ask the volunteer to clarify only the facts needed to choose the correct route from the sources; do not add new questions beyond what the sources use.\n\n---\n\nHOW TO DECIDE THE PHASE\n- If the current or previous user message is the first about this situation (e.g. \"Applicant says: My employer hasn't paid...\") → Phase 1. Output Part A + Part B only.\n- If the conversation shows the volunteer already received Part A and Part B and is now providing the applicant's answers (e.g. \"Applicant answered: still employed, last payment 2 months ago, amount $X...\") → Phase 2. Output Part C only.\n\n---\n\nCRITICAL RULES (do not sway)\n- Base every part (A, B, C) ONLY on the text in the sources below. Do not invent questions, routes, or legal conclusions.\n- For Part B: only after a **concrete** Selected Entry ID matches the situation. Then include every triage question for **that** entry (e.g. all items in triage_questions). Never output Part B questions from a different legal area than the user's situation.\n- For Part C: apply the source branching conditions exactly. If branching logic says \"if Qx = ... then route ...\", follow that condition literally and do not skip checks.\n- If a source has **`guardrail`** text (for example \"Must NEVER ...\" / \"You MAY ...\"), obey it strictly for the Selected Entry and prefer refusal + safe routing whenever the request would violate that guardrail.\n- Do not add generic disclaimers, \"jurisdictional\" caveats, or extra questions—**except** in the **No safe match (Unclear)** path, where you must briefly explain the retrieval/situation mismatch. Do not add routes that are not in the sources.\n- Cite every fact with the source name in square brackets. Do not combine sources.\n- Respect the guardrail in the sources (e.g. \"Must NOT advise on...\"). Do not do anything the guardrail forbids.\n- If the sources block is empty, say only: \"No relevant guidance was found in the available sources for this situation.\"\n- If the sources block is non-empty but **none** of the entries match after a full scan (including `user_query` / `variations`), use the **No safe match (Phase 1)** format—do not list unrelated triage questions.\n- If the sources **do** contain a matching entry for the volunteer’s words, you **must** use that entry's id in **Selected Entry:** with Part A and Part B from that entry—not Unclear—**even if** other entries also appear in the same context.\n- Answer in English only.\nEach image source has the document file name in the top left corner of the image with coordinates (10,10) pixels with format <filename.ext#page=N>,\nand the image figure name is right-aligned in the top right corner of the image.\nThe filename of the actual image is in the top right corner of the image and is in the format <figureN_N.png>.\nEach text source starts in a new line and has the file name followed by colon and the actual information\nAlways include the source document filename for each fact you use in the response in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N].\nIf you are referencing an image, add the image filename in the format: [document_name.ext#page=N(image_name.png)].\n\nPossible citations for current question:  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8(figure8_1.png)]  [Financial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Are interest rates high?\n\n\nSources:\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=7:  This section examines the correlations between stock indices, cryptocurrency prices, and commodity prices, revealing how changes in one market can have ripple effects across the financial ecosystem.### Impact of Macroeconomic Factors   <figure><figcaption>Impact of Interest Rates, Inflation, and GDP Growth on Financial Markets<br>The image is a line graph titled \"on Financial Markets\" displaying data from 2018 to 2023. It tracks three variables: Interest Rates %, Inflation Data %, and GDP Growth %, each represented by a different colored line (blue for Interest Rates, orange for Inflation Data, and gray for GDP Growth). Interest Rates % start around 2% in 2018, dip to about 0.25% in 2021, then rise to 1.5% in 2023. Inflation Data % begin at approximately 1.9% in 2018, rise to a peak near 3.4% in 2022, and then decrease to 2.5% in 2023. GDP Growth % shows significant fluctuations, starting at 3% in 2018, plunging to almost -4% in 2020, then rebounding to around 4.5% in 2021 before gradually declining to around 2.8% in 2023.</figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=8: </figcaption></figure>   Macroeconomic factors such as interest rates, inflation, and GDP growth play a pivotal role in shaping financial markets. This section analyzes how these factors have influenced stock, cryptocurrency, and commodity markets over recent years, providing insights into the complex relationship between the economy and financial market performance.## Future Predictions and Trends   <figure><figcaption>Relative Growth Trends for S&P 500, Bitcoin, and Oil Prices (2024 Indexed to 100)<br>This horizontal bar chart shows prices indexed to 100 for the years 2024 to 2028. It compares the prices of Oil, Bitcoin, and the S&P 500 across these years. In 2024, all three have an index value of 100. From 2025 to 2028, all three generally increase, with Bitcoin consistently having the highest index value, followed closely by the S&P 500 and then Oil. The chart uses grey bars for Oil, orange bars for Bitcoin, and blue bars for the S&P 500.</figcaption></figure>   Based on historical data, current trends, and economic indicators, this section presents predictions for the future of financial markets.\n\nFinancial Market Analysis Report 2023.pdf#page=2: advanced data analytics to present a clear picture of the complex interplay between different financial markets and their potential trajectories## Introduction to Financial Markets   <figure><figcaption>Global Financial Market Distribution (2023)<br>The pie chart features four categories: Stocks, Bonds, Cryptocurrencies, and Commodities. Stocks take up the largest portion of the chart, represented in blue, accounting for 40%. Bonds are the second largest, shown in orange, making up 25%. Cryptocurrencies are depicted in gray and cover 20% of the chart. Commodities are the smallest segment, shown in yellow, comprising 15%.</figcaption></figure>   The global financial market is a vast and intricate network of exchanges, instruments, and assets, ranging from traditional stocks and bonds to modern cryptocurrencies and commodities. Each segment plays a crucial role in the overall economy, and their interactions can have profound effects on global financial stability. This section provides an overview of these segments and sets the stage for a detailed analysis## Stock Market Overview   <figure><figcaption><br>The image is a line graph titled \"5-Year Trend of the S&P 500 Index."}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z/C/HgAGgwJ/lK3Q6wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==", "detail": "auto"}}]}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt"}}], "followup_questions": null, "answer": null}, "session_state": null}
